WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.202 --> 00:00:02.765
﻿Okay.

00:00:02.796 --> 00:00:03.959
Let’s start.

00:00:03.984 --> 00:00:05.999
Hello everyone.
Greetings.

00:00:06.424 --> 00:00:09.445
Good evening or good morning.

00:00:10.156 --> 00:00:14.885
Steve, I wanted to offer the difference
between paper and electronic books

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:17.804
as the topic for today’s discussion.

00:00:17.829 --> 00:00:22.718
However, in the process of preparation
I understood that…

00:00:22.743 --> 00:00:27.006
I will also be asking questions about books
and the Internet.

00:00:27.031 --> 00:00:31.188
So, we are counterpoising books
and the Internet. All right?

00:00:31.213 --> 00:00:32.651
Okay.

00:00:32.676 --> 00:00:37.376
I think it will be interesting for our viewers.

00:00:37.645 --> 00:00:41.491
I’ve prepared several questions.
The first one is:

00:00:41.516 --> 00:00:44.116
Steve, what is reading?

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:48.380
What is your view on reading?
What does it mean?

00:00:49.604 --> 00:00:51.604
What is reading?

00:00:52.209 --> 00:00:56.741
I think reading is the most efficient way of…

00:01:02.379 --> 00:01:04.129
How to say it?

00:01:04.249 --> 00:01:06.874
Communication?
Sharing?

00:01:07.494 --> 00:01:13.381
No, not communication. I think talking
is the best way of communication.

00:01:13.406 --> 00:01:14.969
But…

00:01:17.045 --> 00:01:24.315
[Reading is the best way] to learn something.
Of course, one can learn from experience.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:27.440
It can teach us a lot.

00:01:29.264 --> 00:01:37.364
In the end, it’s our experience
that helps us learn something.

00:01:38.008 --> 00:01:47.108
But it’s impossible
to gain experience in everything.

00:01:48.838 --> 00:01:57.438
Through reading, we can partake
in many situations and experiences.

00:02:00.715 --> 00:02:07.315
I’m sorry, my Russian is…
I haven’t spoken Russian for a long time.

00:02:09.194 --> 00:02:15.094
For instance, when I’m reading a book…

00:02:15.218 --> 00:02:20.818
Let's say it's a 19th century novel.

00:02:21.964 --> 00:02:27.053
I cannot personally experience
life in the 19th century,

00:02:27.078 --> 00:02:32.108
but I can do this via the book.

00:02:35.383 --> 00:02:40.883
Let’s say I’ve bought a computer.
How to use a computer?

00:02:41.877 --> 00:02:46.495
I can look it up on Youtube.

00:02:47.902 --> 00:03:00.702
But if I have the choice between
watching a video and reading, I prefer reading.

00:03:00.845 --> 00:03:08.045
It is faster, and I can re-read it
whenever I want to.

00:03:10.753 --> 00:03:15.284
It is convenient, efficient, and economical…

00:03:16.729 --> 00:03:19.229
- Economical?
 - Yes.

00:03:21.155 --> 00:03:23.968
In my view, it is the most…

00:03:24.707 --> 00:03:30.539
We have computers
and various other devices.

00:03:33.648 --> 00:03:37.191
However, the very idea of reading
and writing...

00:03:38.591 --> 00:03:48.991
I can communicate something to you via reading.

00:03:50.357 --> 00:03:52.525
- Pass something to me.
- Yes.

00:03:52.550 --> 00:03:54.511
This is fantastic.

00:03:54.536 --> 00:04:02.036
The very fact that I’m reading,
and all these countless letters

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:08.530
turn into something meaningful…

00:04:10.057 --> 00:04:17.757
To me, this is an amazing way of communication,

00:04:19.281 --> 00:04:21.781
teaching and…

00:04:23.353 --> 00:04:24.916
education.

00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:28.940
- Education.
- Yes.

00:04:29.002 --> 00:04:33.602
This is the most efficient means of education.

00:04:35.031 --> 00:04:41.831
Besides, reading gives you pleasure.
People who love poetry, novels,

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:51.650
or love reading history books, like myself…

00:04:53.383 --> 00:04:58.183
I’m reading books on the history of Central Asia.

00:04:59.615 --> 00:05:03.346
I can…

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:13.270
- You can learn a lot from them. 
- I can learn a lot from them. It’s efficient.

00:05:13.411 --> 00:05:17.370
Reading is sacred.
It’s a great thing.

00:05:21.113 --> 00:05:26.413
Both in Russia and Canada,
some people are poor readers.

00:05:28.748 --> 00:05:46.048
Your level of reading is the main indicator
of the amount of success in your life.

00:05:52.546 --> 00:05:58.166
Statistically, people who are passionate readers…
not all, but many of them

00:05:58.191 --> 00:06:07.491
tend to be more successful professionally,
academically etc.

00:06:09.450 --> 00:06:15.750
Every country has about 20% of people
who don’t read that well…

00:06:17.906 --> 00:06:22.695
We say “don’t read too much.”
I’ve got you, Steve.

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:29.220
With your permission, I will quote
philosopher Andriy Baumeister.

00:06:29.789 --> 00:06:33.331
He is very popular in Ukraine now.

00:06:33.525 --> 00:06:41.125
When asked about reading, he said, “As we read,
we are deciphering symbols,

00:06:41.696 --> 00:06:44.103
which requires concentration.

00:06:44.128 --> 00:06:47.647
Reading is the art of deciphering signs.

00:06:47.672 --> 00:06:53.868
One can fulfill his or her potential if he or she
has the codes to these sign systems.

00:06:53.893 --> 00:06:57.435
May I ask your opinion about this?

00:06:58.413 --> 00:07:04.636
I am especially interested in it,
because you read in several scripts,

00:07:04.661 --> 00:07:09.142
including Persian, Arabic, hieroglyphs etc.

00:07:10.047 --> 00:07:12.968
It would be interesting
to hear your opinion,

00:07:12.993 --> 00:07:18.416
since you can view it from
standpoints of various sign systems.

00:07:18.441 --> 00:07:25.141
Alphabets, like Cyrillic or Latin ones,
are sign systems.

00:07:25.357 --> 00:07:30.570
Arabic is also an alphabet,
even though it’s a bit complicated.

00:07:30.595 --> 00:07:33.825
And then there are hieroglyphs…

00:07:35.854 --> 00:07:42.154
To me, these are systems of writing
you have to adjust yourself to.

00:07:48.072 --> 00:08:03.972
Our brain has the system for construction of…
a model, or patterns.

00:08:05.803 --> 00:08:08.721
We have this word. Patterns.

00:08:09.044 --> 00:08:16.144
We can do this with Chinese hieroglyphs
as well as with Latin alphabet.

00:08:16.935 --> 00:08:25.035
Of course, the more we use a particular sign system,
the more comfortable we get.

00:08:25.442 --> 00:08:33.186
I can read Latin script better than Cyrillic one,
even though Cyrillic is not that complicated.

00:08:33.211 --> 00:08:43.011
Still, I can read Polish, although my Polish
is worse than my Russian.

00:08:44.203 --> 00:08:47.985
Cyrillic alphabet is more complicated to me…

00:08:48.010 --> 00:08:51.052
Arabic and Chinese are even more difficult.

00:08:51.077 --> 00:08:56.430
It depends on how much you’ve read
in this sign system before.

00:08:56.455 --> 00:09:04.455
You have to let your brain get used to it,
and your brain will gradually adjust,

00:09:14.371 --> 00:09:20.671
regardless of whether it’s Latin, Korean,
Cyrillic, or Chinese.

00:09:21.495 --> 00:09:36.495
In fact, we didn't read in the first hundreds of
thousands of years of our existence as animals.

00:09:38.866 --> 00:09:42.811
And now we are capable of doing it.

00:09:42.973 --> 00:09:54.773
The fact that our brain has this ability nowadays
is a huge step forward.

00:09:56.194 --> 00:10:10.144
A great event in our development as…

00:10:11.018 --> 00:10:13.143
As living beings.

00:10:13.190 --> 00:10:14.752
As species.

00:10:17.395 --> 00:10:20.627
Okay, moving forward to the next question.

00:10:20.652 --> 00:10:28.652
Do you think there is any difference between
reading paper and electronic books?

00:10:31.511 --> 00:10:38.211
Do you regard reading exclusively paper books
as your ultimate goal?

00:10:41.872 --> 00:10:44.893
I prefer reading paper books.

00:10:46.257 --> 00:10:53.957
When I’m reading for pleasure, for instance,
in English, French, Russian etc.,

00:10:55.289 --> 00:11:08.789
where at least 95% of words
are familiar to me. It's a minimum.

00:11:11.277 --> 00:11:18.477
If every page contains more than
5 unfamiliar words,

00:11:20.677 --> 00:11:24.845
it is not pleasant, and I don’t like it.

00:11:26.795 --> 00:11:33.395
Even if it does not impact my understanding,
it is still annoying.

00:11:33.648 --> 00:11:36.944
I don’t know what this word means.

00:11:36.969 --> 00:11:46.369
This is why, when I read in the languages
I’m not fluent in,

00:11:46.393 --> 00:11:49.693
I prefer reading on my iPad,

00:11:50.617 --> 00:11:54.092
which allows to search for words or phrases

00:11:54.117 --> 00:11:56.340
and listen to their pronunciation.

00:11:56.465 --> 00:12:00.932
For instance, stresses in Russian or
pronunciation in Arabic,

00:12:00.957 --> 00:12:10.357
where spelling has nothing to do
with pronunciation.

00:12:11.226 --> 00:12:16.526
Reading electronic books on iPad
has many advantages.

00:12:26.528 --> 00:12:33.586
However, reading is reading.
I love paper books...

00:12:36.939 --> 00:12:42.739
Currently, we are unable to travel
due to Covid.

00:12:45.007 --> 00:12:49.607
But whenever I’m traveling,
I prefer the iPad.

00:12:49.894 --> 00:12:52.060
The iPad instead of paper books?

00:12:53.485 --> 00:12:56.716
Right, because I don’t want to…

00:13:01.156 --> 00:13:05.115
carry two or three suitcases of books.

00:13:05.709 --> 00:13:11.509
I love reading. If I'd carried suitcases
of books with me,

00:13:13.483 --> 00:13:21.783
and then bought more books in Kyiv or wherever,
my luggage would've been very heavy.

00:13:25.903 --> 00:13:32.503
This is why iPad is very convenient.
I can buy any book instantly.

00:13:34.395 --> 00:13:39.195
When I find an interesting book on the Internet,

00:13:39.746 --> 00:13:48.746
I can buy it in English, Russian, or any other language
and start reading it.

00:13:49.971 --> 00:13:52.046
This is very convenient.

00:13:52.071 --> 00:13:57.468
However, reading for the sake of reading
is better when it’s a hard copy.

00:13:57.493 --> 00:14:00.410
Paper books, then. Okay.

00:14:02.202 --> 00:14:07.202
Why do we even need paper books
in the information era?

00:14:08.328 --> 00:14:09.940
Let me explain.

00:14:09.965 --> 00:14:18.765
In the 1990s, at least in the post-Soviet countries,
many teachers and scholars predicted

00:14:20.046 --> 00:14:26.646
that books will become obsolete and that,
instead of reading books,

00:14:27.246 --> 00:14:31.315
people of the future will watch videos
or listen to audios,

00:14:31.340 --> 00:14:34.200
that is, receive information via different channels.

00:14:34.225 --> 00:14:39.325
Nevertheless, as we see, books are doing just fine.

00:14:40.495 --> 00:14:44.975
What are the explanations, in your opinion?

00:14:47.840 --> 00:14:50.652
I think that we need books,

00:14:55.150 --> 00:15:00.550
because in today’s life,
there are many… distractions.

00:15:03.475 --> 00:15:06.288
Distractions, distractions.

00:15:16.043 --> 00:15:19.758
Of course, there are both advantages
and disadvantages.

00:15:19.783 --> 00:15:25.883
We can instantly look something up
or find out what happened.

00:15:28.321 --> 00:15:32.770
There are countless distractions, that’s right.

00:15:32.795 --> 00:15:37.995
However, when you sit down and start reading a book,

00:15:39.307 --> 00:15:43.578
it’s only you, the book, and its content.

00:15:49.818 --> 00:15:57.218
You find yourself in another world,
in a different situation, and you can…

00:16:04.211 --> 00:16:10.811
Instead of sitting there, you are already
in a different universe.

00:16:11.701 --> 00:16:18.701
You cannot click on a link.
It’s just a book, and you have to read it.

00:16:19.581 --> 00:16:24.113
This is why I think books are irreplaceable.

00:16:28.961 --> 00:16:32.995
Of course, there are videos, iPads, whatever.

00:16:33.020 --> 00:16:39.418
There will be artificial intelligence
or something else, I don’t know.

00:16:39.443 --> 00:16:49.043
Still, reading paper books
is a crucial activity.

00:16:51.146 --> 00:16:54.689
In my view, we absolutely need it.

00:16:57.479 --> 00:17:03.279
After several years of studying
a foreign language with Lingq,

00:17:05.711 --> 00:17:08.845
I’m still impressed
by the fact that…

00:17:08.870 --> 00:17:12.723
Studying with Lingq always implies reading.

00:17:12.748 --> 00:17:19.075
I feel that, in the course of the past years,
Lingq has helped me concentrate better.

00:17:19.100 --> 00:17:23.470
Reading helps me concentrate
and develops my imagination,

00:17:23.495 --> 00:17:28.040
because, as you’ve mentioned,
reading a book helps you escape from reality

00:17:28.065 --> 00:17:31.190
and travel to a different era.

00:17:33.858 --> 00:17:39.438
As for me, it’s an interesting
thought that, on the Internet,

00:17:39.463 --> 00:17:42.498
knowledge is always fragmented.

00:17:42.523 --> 00:17:44.666
A little bit here, a little bit there.

00:17:44.691 --> 00:17:50.573
In its turn, a book, especially a massive one,
offers you a narrative.

00:17:50.598 --> 00:17:53.938
There are no distractions,
you cannot click anywhere,

00:17:53.963 --> 00:17:59.363
and you embark on this long,
concentrated journey.

00:17:59.582 --> 00:18:01.036
This is my view.

00:18:01.061 --> 00:18:02.811
Yeah, I agree.

00:18:02.999 --> 00:18:10.099
After I read a book, I look at my library
and remember it with pleasure.

00:18:16.282 --> 00:18:18.907
It’s a great pleasure.
I love books.

00:18:20.094 --> 00:18:22.802
As for studying languages,

00:18:26.854 --> 00:18:32.754
I’m not eager to read books with
numerous unfamiliar words.

00:18:33.049 --> 00:18:34.028
This is why…

00:18:34.053 --> 00:18:36.154
- It’s annoying.
- Yeah.

00:18:36.179 --> 00:18:39.929
This is why… I want… how do you say…

00:18:42.930 --> 00:18:48.630
Increase my level in order to be able
to read paper books.

00:18:52.819 --> 00:18:56.014
From your experience, how many…

00:18:56.039 --> 00:19:00.135
I know this number would differ
from language to language,

00:19:00.160 --> 00:19:05.699
but how many words should there be
in your passive vocabulary

00:19:05.724 --> 00:19:08.252
so that you could read paper books?

00:19:08.277 --> 00:19:09.902
A lot. A lot.

00:19:13.160 --> 00:19:14.785
This depends…

00:19:15.435 --> 00:19:21.835
You know, in Lingq, we read all the dictionary forms
of the word.

00:19:28.788 --> 00:19:33.688
For Russian, it might be about 60 thousand words.

00:19:35.649 --> 00:19:39.198
For English, it’s 40 thousand or so.

00:19:39.223 --> 00:19:45.023
I don’t know, but it’s a huge number.
A whole lot of words.

00:19:45.221 --> 00:19:54.021
For instance, a person
who knows Slovak language

00:19:55.076 --> 00:19:59.451
can read in other
Slavic languages as well.

00:20:02.640 --> 00:20:06.651
He or she can guess the meaning
without actually knowing the language.

00:20:06.676 --> 00:20:08.101
- That’s right.
- Yeah.

00:20:08.126 --> 00:20:12.606
Someone who knows Italian can read Spanish.

00:20:13.109 --> 00:20:19.709
In order to read Russian, someone who knows
another Slavic language

00:20:21.061 --> 00:20:24.631
would need a lot… A lot of words.

00:20:24.656 --> 00:20:26.148
Okay.

00:20:26.173 --> 00:20:29.091
Steve, don’t you think that…

00:20:29.598 --> 00:20:39.498
Firstly, people tend to read less nowadays
than 30 years ago.

00:20:40.248 --> 00:20:46.326
If we take the case of Canada,
do people still attend public libraries?

00:20:46.351 --> 00:20:52.266
Are public libraries or bookshops with paper books
still popular?

00:20:52.291 --> 00:20:54.916
I can’t say for sure.

00:20:56.057 --> 00:20:58.682
Libraries are popular.

00:21:00.044 --> 00:21:07.544
People attend libraries, and there are numerous
activities surrounding them.

00:21:09.125 --> 00:21:15.825
People read, attend meetings, discussions,
and do many other things.

00:21:18.791 --> 00:21:22.958
Libraries are great, they are very good.

00:21:23.539 --> 00:21:29.439
As for bookstores, unfortunately,
there are enormous chains…

00:21:39.566 --> 00:21:43.183
- We say ‘networks.’
- Yeah, networks.

00:21:43.208 --> 00:21:47.064
Small bookstores don’t exist anymore.

00:21:53.838 --> 00:22:00.654
Especially now, with the COVID situation,
there are few of them left.

00:22:00.679 --> 00:22:05.679
There are some in Vancouver,
but I rarely go there.

00:22:09.178 --> 00:22:15.417
Only if it’s absolutely necessary to buy something,
I go to the bookshop.

00:22:15.442 --> 00:22:19.088
But I wouldln’t… I forgot the word.

00:22:24.810 --> 00:22:29.342
Nevertheless, people still visit bookstores.

00:22:30.567 --> 00:22:34.838
A year ago, many people used to go there.

00:22:38.816 --> 00:22:43.916
I mean big bookshops,
like the three-storeyed ones.

00:22:44.205 --> 00:22:49.805
They have cafes, tables…
But these are chain bookstores.

00:22:53.993 --> 00:22:58.473
I think it’s the same in Ukraine, isn’t it?

00:23:00.115 --> 00:23:05.654
Well, statistics says that we read
less than we used to.

00:23:05.679 --> 00:23:08.179
Especially children.

00:23:09.120 --> 00:23:14.551
Since I’m a father now, I know that many
parents are struggling

00:23:14.576 --> 00:23:19.676
to accustom their children
to books instead of this.

00:23:20.325 --> 00:23:22.950
This is a huge issue.

00:23:23.766 --> 00:23:28.666
Do you think people will read more in the future?

00:23:29.354 --> 00:23:32.897
Will there be a rollback to books?

00:23:33.965 --> 00:23:38.496
Or will gadgets and virtual reality prevail?

00:23:42.635 --> 00:23:49.335
First of all, one should be able to read
in order to use the iPhone.

00:23:51.959 --> 00:23:55.605
It requires a little bit of reading.

00:23:56.738 --> 00:24:05.822
I see that my grandchildren…
I have five grandkids.

00:24:06.463 --> 00:24:09.171
They do read. All of them.

00:24:11.200 --> 00:24:15.575
Whenever I buy them books, they read them.

00:24:21.692 --> 00:24:26.692
Sometimes it’s hard copies,
sometimes it’s Kindle.

00:24:28.000 --> 00:24:29.625
They do read,

00:24:31.970 --> 00:24:36.241
as well as my son and my daughter-in-law.

00:24:38.949 --> 00:24:44.449
Oh my God, I can’t even name
these relatives in Russian.

00:24:47.923 --> 00:24:53.323
Daughter-in-law is probably “nevestka.”
The son’s wife.

00:24:54.603 --> 00:24:58.606
The viewers will write the
correct word in the comments.

00:24:58.631 --> 00:25:00.446
I’m not a specialist.

00:25:00.471 --> 00:25:05.571
I think that in our society,
as well as in Ukraine,

00:25:07.660 --> 00:25:12.760
there are people who read
and those who don’t read.

00:25:13.003 --> 00:25:18.803
Some people love reading,
and others don’t read that much.

00:25:18.835 --> 00:25:23.106
In my view, it all depends on the school.

00:25:26.211 --> 00:25:37.111
It is crucial to help children
read well and fast.

00:25:38.865 --> 00:25:44.265
Teaching children to read
is the most important thing.

00:25:48.656 --> 00:25:53.956
Those people who haven't learned
to read well at school…

00:25:57.810 --> 00:26:05.110
They can also be good workers.

00:26:05.134 --> 00:26:11.234
I’ve worked with many people at our plants.

00:26:13.449 --> 00:26:20.549
These people are very good with machinery,
and they aren’t keen readers.

00:26:20.605 --> 00:26:23.977
You might call them professionally successful.

00:26:24.002 --> 00:26:25.629
They have jobs.

00:26:25.654 --> 00:26:33.054
However, according to statistics,
poor readers are less successful in life.

00:26:34.027 --> 00:26:38.617
This is not a rule.
One can read a lot and be unsuccessful.

00:26:38.642 --> 00:26:41.267
But the statistics are…

00:26:45.919 --> 00:26:49.800
Steve, what do you think about reading several books
at the same time?

00:26:49.825 --> 00:26:56.013
Do you read several books simultaneously
in different languages?

00:26:56.038 --> 00:26:57.743
Yeah, it depends.

00:26:57.768 --> 00:26:59.643
Right now, I’m…

00:27:03.213 --> 00:27:06.465
I’m studying both Arabic and Persian.

00:27:06.490 --> 00:27:09.927
Sometimes I read…
I have it here.

00:27:11.551 --> 00:27:13.551
Сool!
Good.

00:27:14.784 --> 00:27:18.809
It’s not my only Persian book.

00:27:21.336 --> 00:27:23.461
Is it a handbook?

00:27:23.746 --> 00:27:27.289
It’s a handbook.
[I also read] mini stories.

00:27:30.395 --> 00:27:32.463
You have to read it again and again,

00:27:32.488 --> 00:27:38.288
because you can’t memorize
everything from the first time.

00:27:38.402 --> 00:27:42.363
Sometimes I read in Persian,
and sometimes in Arabic.

00:27:42.388 --> 00:27:44.993
But I can’t do this with novels.

00:27:45.018 --> 00:27:49.549
If I’m reading a novel or a history book,

00:27:51.632 --> 00:27:54.257
I have to concentrate…

00:27:55.347 --> 00:27:56.729
So you read in one language?

00:27:56.754 --> 00:27:58.629
Yeah, one book.

00:27:59.634 --> 00:28:02.343
Okay, this is interesting.

00:28:02.561 --> 00:28:07.620
Does it matter to you whether
it’s fiction or non-fiction?

00:28:07.645 --> 00:28:11.876
And does it matter for language learners as well?

00:28:11.901 --> 00:28:16.626
It’s a matter of taste.

00:28:17.826 --> 00:28:20.451
I prefer non-fiction.

00:28:20.821 --> 00:28:27.201
To me, history is particularly interesting.

00:28:29.477 --> 00:28:35.977
I’m studying Persian,
and I will read books on the history of Iran

00:28:36.651 --> 00:28:39.832
or the history of Central Asia.

00:28:39.857 --> 00:28:45.557
It’s all very interesting.
The influence of Iran on India…

00:28:48.642 --> 00:28:50.204
On India.

00:28:51.582 --> 00:28:58.582
Or the relations between Turkish peoples and cultures
and Iranian ones.

00:29:00.205 --> 00:29:02.795
It’s all very interesting.

00:29:02.820 --> 00:29:05.436
This is why I prefer reading…

00:29:05.461 --> 00:29:08.118
I want to know lots of things.

00:29:08.143 --> 00:29:09.705
And…

00:29:13.115 --> 00:29:18.815
As for fiction, I prefer reading it in foreign languages.

00:29:19.838 --> 00:29:21.677
I love Tolstoy.

00:29:21.702 --> 00:29:24.900
Russians usually say, “Oh, you shouldn’t read it,

00:29:24.925 --> 00:29:31.125
it’s written in old-fashioned language,
and it won’t help you.”

00:29:32.892 --> 00:29:35.100
I totally disagree.

00:29:35.125 --> 00:29:36.688
Me too.

00:29:38.641 --> 00:29:45.041
I think that, when we are studying a language,
it’s a bit unreal to us.

00:29:46.358 --> 00:29:49.921
The real one is our native language.

00:29:49.946 --> 00:29:55.246
This is why I love reading about
19th century Russia.

00:29:55.450 --> 00:30:00.581
Another language, another world,
era, and unfamiliar situations -

00:30:00.606 --> 00:30:04.669
all of these are parts of the pleasure.

00:30:06.790 --> 00:30:14.890
This is why I read fiction, like fairy tales etc.,
in French, Italian, or Russian.

00:30:19.084 --> 00:30:23.784
It’s very…
In English, I only read non-fiction.

00:30:26.178 --> 00:30:27.741
Okay.

00:30:27.963 --> 00:30:33.863
I wanted to add that the novel
I’ve recently read in Italian

00:30:34.238 --> 00:30:38.405
was written by an author from Barcelona.

00:30:39.665 --> 00:30:45.365
I have to mention that the language of fiction
is amazing.

00:30:46.588 --> 00:30:51.460
All these beautiful phrases, comparisons,
synonyms, and so on.

00:30:51.485 --> 00:30:56.085
Besides, in fiction (we call it belle lettre)

00:30:59.016 --> 00:31:00.991
there are always dialogues between the characters.

00:31:01.016 --> 00:31:03.989
They communicate, and it’s quite helpful,

00:31:04.014 --> 00:31:08.206
whereas non-fiction gives you
only facts, facts, and facts.

00:31:08.231 --> 00:31:11.877
This is why it’s a matter of taste.

00:31:16.346 --> 00:31:18.971
Okay, one more question…

00:31:20.295 --> 00:31:22.920
Yes, I agree with you.

00:31:23.829 --> 00:31:30.629
For instance, Tolstoy was among the first books
I’ve read in Russian.

00:31:32.498 --> 00:31:40.498
It was “The Kreutzer Sonata.”
It’s a small book in comparison to “War and Peace.”

00:31:41.375 --> 00:31:46.175
Subsequently, I’ve read his other books as well.

00:31:46.851 --> 00:31:50.601
I love the way he uses the language.

00:31:53.544 --> 00:31:58.421
Tolstoy is my favorite author.
Perhaps it’s not too original, but…

00:31:58.446 --> 00:32:03.746
To me, it’s quite an easy and
comprehensible reading.

00:32:09.146 --> 00:32:13.515
Unlike Dostoevsky, who is just… complicated,

00:32:13.540 --> 00:32:17.707
Tolstoy is all about clarity and beauty.

00:32:19.154 --> 00:32:20.717
I agree.

00:32:20.741 --> 00:32:23.127
I love Chekhov as well.

00:32:23.152 --> 00:32:29.852
Me too. Chekhov has some short stories,
which is good for language learners.

00:32:36.706 --> 00:32:42.406
We don’t like reading lots of text.
His works are concise.

00:32:43.627 --> 00:32:50.027
Short stories are very attractive to us
when we study a language.

00:32:51.754 --> 00:32:58.354
Steve, there are the notions of ‘speed reading’
 and ‘slow reading.’

00:32:59.126 --> 00:33:03.042
I don’t know about Canada, but in Ukraine,

00:33:03.067 --> 00:33:08.133
courses of ‘speed reading’ have been quite popular
for a while.

00:33:08.158 --> 00:33:12.203
Reading quickly, diagonally,
consuming more information,

00:33:12.228 --> 00:33:16.708
reading more books within shorter time etc.

00:33:17.766 --> 00:33:21.523
What is your opinion about it?
Don’t we sacrifice the quality,

00:33:21.548 --> 00:33:24.610
especially with regard to studying
a foreign language?

00:33:24.635 --> 00:33:28.906
Should we go in for speed reading at all?

00:33:32.354 --> 00:33:38.054
Well… I’ve tried it once.
I didn’t find it very efficient.

00:33:39.673 --> 00:33:44.222
In my view, our reading speed increases
with the amount of books we read.

00:33:44.247 --> 00:33:46.872
One should read a lot.

00:33:47.855 --> 00:33:51.605
Maybe I’m old-fashioned, maybe I am.

00:33:52.400 --> 00:33:56.150
But I don’t have patience, for instance,

00:34:04.197 --> 00:34:06.572
to study mnemonics…

00:34:08.380 --> 00:34:13.080
Mnemonics. It’s a method for memorizing things.

00:34:13.434 --> 00:34:20.134
I’d better spend my time reading,
memorizing, forgetting, whatever.

00:34:21.306 --> 00:34:30.406
If I see these words often,
I will memorize them.

00:34:32.624 --> 00:34:38.653
For example, if I don’t read or speak Russian that much,
I will forget it.

00:34:38.678 --> 00:34:43.578
If I start speaking or reading again,
it will improve…

00:34:47.624 --> 00:34:52.624
This is why I rarely use speed reading, mnemonics,

00:34:54.615 --> 00:34:57.532
or spaced repetition systems.

00:35:02.186 --> 00:35:07.786
I’m not saying it doesn’t work.
It might work just fine.

00:35:07.792 --> 00:35:13.492
I’ve tried spaced repetition, mnemonics,
and speed reading.

00:35:15.708 --> 00:35:19.115
I’ve bought the books and blah-blah-blah…

00:35:19.140 --> 00:35:23.410
But I haven’t… how do you say “continue.”

00:35:28.896 --> 00:35:33.064
Yeah, you can say “I haven’t continued.”

00:35:33.743 --> 00:35:36.243
I haven’t continued.

00:35:36.361 --> 00:35:39.174
Yes, let’s put it this way.

00:35:40.131 --> 00:35:43.990
Reading is a powerful tool for memorizing.

00:35:44.015 --> 00:35:47.973
Why has humanity “forgotten” about it?

00:35:47.999 --> 00:35:53.699
Why do people keep inventing
new systems, mnemonics etc.?

00:35:53.770 --> 00:35:59.829
Reading exists for many centuries.
Have we forgotten about it?

00:35:59.854 --> 00:36:02.354
I think it’s normal.

00:36:02.739 --> 00:36:06.697
People will always invent new systems.

00:36:08.878 --> 00:36:22.178
Systems for faster running,
for healthier eating…

00:36:23.500 --> 00:36:28.100
People are coming up with all sorts of things.

00:36:29.313 --> 00:36:32.958
However, reading will always exist.

00:36:37.374 --> 00:36:41.229
For me, electronic books are very convenient.

00:36:44.693 --> 00:36:50.393
They give us one more option
with regard to the situation.

00:36:51.620 --> 00:36:57.120
For instance, I always read on my Kindle or iPad
while traveling,

00:36:59.172 --> 00:37:04.047
because it’s convenient for studying languages…
a language.

00:37:04.072 --> 00:37:06.072
It’s convenient.

00:37:07.506 --> 00:37:09.381
One more thing:

00:37:10.097 --> 00:37:14.997
we can use the same content in many ways,
in many…

00:37:30.621 --> 00:37:33.356
Approaches, different ways.

00:37:33.381 --> 00:37:36.006
Different approaches.

00:37:37.161 --> 00:37:40.955
To me, reading paper books,
reading on my iPad,

00:37:40.980 --> 00:37:47.080
listening to podcasts or audiobooks
are the available options.

00:37:50.195 --> 00:37:53.945
If we use all these different types…

00:37:57.036 --> 00:38:01.877
I don’t know,
some people prefer reading paper books,

00:38:01.902 --> 00:38:06.782
others like electronic books
or use all of these [methods of reading].

00:38:06.807 --> 00:38:09.432
Variety is good for us.

00:38:12.688 --> 00:38:15.598
Variety is very beneficial for us.

00:38:15.623 --> 00:38:17.185
Okay.

00:38:17.406 --> 00:38:19.031
Variety, yes.

00:38:20.675 --> 00:38:24.719
Steve, as you know, I popularize
Lingq and your method.

00:38:24.744 --> 00:38:28.400
Different people who want to learn
various languages contact me.

00:38:28.425 --> 00:38:34.001
Sometimes, when we start talking,
I find out that a person

00:38:34.026 --> 00:38:36.903
has no habit of reading even in Russian.

00:38:36.928 --> 00:38:40.991
They say, “I don’t read. I just don’t.”

00:38:42.586 --> 00:38:48.863
Are there any ways to inspire people
and urge them to develop this habit?

00:38:48.888 --> 00:38:52.124
Many people develop the habit of reading

00:38:52.149 --> 00:38:56.420
after my course and the lessons at Lingq.

00:38:57.182 --> 00:38:59.410
In the beginning, they are quite skeptical:

00:38:59.435 --> 00:39:03.850
“No, I’d rather watch some videos,
series etc.

00:39:03.875 --> 00:39:05.756
Reading is not for me.”

00:39:05.781 --> 00:39:09.427
What would you say to those people?

00:39:11.690 --> 00:39:19.390
First of all, with a video, you have to sit
and watch it for an hour straight.

00:39:22.372 --> 00:39:28.072
This is why videos are not really
convenient or portable…

00:39:29.538 --> 00:39:31.788
We say “portable”.

00:39:33.595 --> 00:39:38.495
Many people dislike reading,
not only in Ukraine.

00:39:42.226 --> 00:39:47.526
Maybe they hated reading at school,
I don’t know.

00:39:49.098 --> 00:39:53.578
In order to learn languages… or a language…

00:39:57.403 --> 00:40:00.409
Both “languages” and “language” are right.

00:40:00.434 --> 00:40:01.997
Okay.

00:40:02.974 --> 00:40:09.574
Reading is the most powerful tool [of learning].
The most powerful.

00:40:12.271 --> 00:40:13.833
But…

00:40:16.735 --> 00:40:24.206
We don’t…
One doesn’t begin with reading.

00:40:26.916 --> 00:40:29.624
One begins with listening.

00:40:32.002 --> 00:40:37.802
I can listen to something
while driving, walking, whatever.

00:40:42.144 --> 00:40:48.344
Listening is the most portable and
convenient way of learning.

00:40:51.392 --> 00:40:55.663
Today, we have mp3 and many other things.

00:40:55.738 --> 00:41:01.438
I have a huge library on my iPhone,
in 15 languages or so.

00:41:07.903 --> 00:41:14.138
Twenty years ago, every school or university
in Ukraine or Canada

00:41:14.163 --> 00:41:19.063
had these huge linguistic libraries
with big tape recorders…

00:41:21.864 --> 00:41:25.456
It’s called “lingaphonic cabinets.”

00:41:25.481 --> 00:41:28.174
There were these big earphones…

00:41:28.199 --> 00:41:32.554
Yeah. Nowadays, all you need are
Airpods and an iPhone.

00:41:32.579 --> 00:41:36.225
Listening is the most portable way.

00:41:36.691 --> 00:41:44.191
When I’m listening, I might understand
20, 30 or 50 percent,

00:41:45.547 --> 00:41:50.871
but I’m eager to read.
I want to know what the content is.

00:41:50.896 --> 00:41:57.496
While I’m listening, my brain is gradually
making some sense of it.

00:42:05.591 --> 00:42:08.985
Reading is the fastest way of learning.

00:42:09.010 --> 00:42:14.963
I listen, then I read it on Lingq
or my iPad.

00:42:14.988 --> 00:42:22.488
I note the unfamiliar words,
and I begin to understand the foreign language.

00:42:27.999 --> 00:42:31.436
But it all starts with listening.

00:42:31.934 --> 00:42:35.736
Then, it’s building up or…
how do you say it?

00:42:35.761 --> 00:42:37.324
Completed.

00:42:37.765 --> 00:42:40.995
It gets completed with reading.

00:42:43.100 --> 00:42:47.943
Probably I won’t be able to
understand everything,

00:42:47.968 --> 00:42:53.068
even if I’ve read and listened to it
numerous times.

00:42:59.375 --> 00:43:03.610
I’ve looked up the words,
noted them down and everything,

00:43:03.635 --> 00:43:07.375
but I still don’t understand
what I’m listening to.

00:43:07.400 --> 00:43:10.622
In this case,
I have to read it once more.

00:43:10.647 --> 00:43:14.502
If I continue this…
virtuous circle.

00:43:20.404 --> 00:43:24.935
Virtuous circle?
I only know “vicious circle.”

00:43:27.232 --> 00:43:31.932
Let’s see… Virtue…
Let’s say “positive circle.”

00:43:35.390 --> 00:43:37.390
Positive circle.

00:43:38.745 --> 00:43:43.252
My brain will eventually get used to this word.

00:43:43.277 --> 00:43:54.277
I think that, if people listen more,
this will arouse their curiosity.

00:43:56.397 --> 00:43:59.022
“What am I listening to?”

00:44:09.699 --> 00:44:12.324
It will push them to read…

00:44:19.572 --> 00:44:23.322
It will encourage them to read more.

00:44:27.066 --> 00:44:30.883
And I think that audiobooks are fantastic.

00:44:30.908 --> 00:44:32.783
They are great.

00:44:32.918 --> 00:44:38.618
In some languages, the quality and
quantity of audiobooks

00:44:39.400 --> 00:44:42.025
are quite impressive.

00:44:42.425 --> 00:44:44.807
It is not the case with Spanish.

00:44:44.832 --> 00:44:50.832
But it is the case with Russian, French,
Swedish, or German.

00:44:51.078 --> 00:44:53.704
I wanted to share something with you.

00:44:53.729 --> 00:45:00.126
Last week, I’ve been preparing a course
for people who learn Turkish.

00:45:00.151 --> 00:45:07.251
I’ve found out that Storytell website features
a huge, enormous library

00:45:09.782 --> 00:45:14.148
in various languages, including Hindi,
Turkish, Arabic, Portuguese…

00:45:14.173 --> 00:45:19.273
A lot of audiobooks.
I will share the link with you.

00:45:19.684 --> 00:45:24.449
They have lots of books in good quality
and with good voice actors.

00:45:24.474 --> 00:45:29.674
Of course, if I'm not fluent
in that language,

00:45:30.221 --> 00:45:35.821
I need to have both the audiobook
and the electronic book

00:45:36.084 --> 00:45:39.355
that can be downloaded by that link.

00:45:39.380 --> 00:45:46.504
If I only listen to an audiobook in Arabic,
it will be a bit complicated.

00:45:46.529 --> 00:45:52.329
Even if I understand 20 percent,
I am curious to know more.

00:45:56.030 --> 00:45:57.856
This is why I’m eager to read.

00:45:57.881 --> 00:46:05.781
Still, as I’ve said before, if a book contains
more than 5% of unfamiliar words,

00:46:07.958 --> 00:46:15.358
I prefer reading an electronic version,
so that I can search the words etc.

00:46:17.890 --> 00:46:20.260
Yeah, this is interesting.

00:46:20.285 --> 00:46:25.485
Steve, can you name three books
in foreign languages

00:46:28.857 --> 00:46:36.257
that have influenced you and your life
and became ingrained in your memory?

00:46:37.076 --> 00:46:41.034
Both fiction and non-fiction are fine.

00:46:41.558 --> 00:46:44.579
It’s hard to name such books…

00:46:46.081 --> 00:46:54.581
Unfortunately, I don’t necessarily remember
the content of the book after I finish it,

00:46:55.195 --> 00:46:57.903
even though I’ve liked it.

00:47:02.550 --> 00:47:08.750
Lately, I’ve been reading books
on Russian influence in India.

00:47:11.441 --> 00:47:14.245
This is very interesting.
Very interesting.

00:47:14.270 --> 00:47:18.525
What is the title of the book?
Are you reading it in Persian?

00:47:18.550 --> 00:47:23.150
It’s in English.
I don’t remember [the title].

00:47:24.624 --> 00:47:27.957
When I’ve been studying Italian…

00:47:30.396 --> 00:47:34.146
I love classics.
“I promessi sposi.”

00:47:35.870 --> 00:47:41.770
When you say to an Italian,
 “I’m reading I promessi sposi”,

00:47:47.679 --> 00:47:53.054
it's the same as with Russian speakers
who studied Tolstoy at schools.

00:47:53.079 --> 00:47:57.979
They don’t like [classical literature].
But I do.

00:47:59.011 --> 00:48:02.345
I love reading Balzac in French.

00:48:06.305 --> 00:48:09.711
- It’s a matter of taste.
- A matter of taste. Great.

00:48:12.351 --> 00:48:15.996
Well, these are all the questions
I have for the moment.

00:48:16.021 --> 00:48:17.616
Thank you so much.

00:48:17.641 --> 00:48:20.438
Thank you, and excuse me for my Russian.

00:48:20.463 --> 00:48:26.263
I will continue studying Persian, Arabic,
and then Turkish.

00:48:27.003 --> 00:48:28.566
And then…

00:48:29.583 --> 00:48:35.383
I just can’t revise…
I can’t spend a lot of time revising.

00:48:42.051 --> 00:48:48.751
My Russian has become a bit worse.
But it will get better with time.

00:48:50.844 --> 00:48:57.633
Steve, I would like to have such conversations
with you at least once a month.

00:48:57.658 --> 00:49:00.073
Maybe we can pick some topics and discuss them…

00:49:00.098 --> 00:49:03.772
Okay. It would be beneficial for my Russian as well.

00:49:03.797 --> 00:49:07.273
This is also good for our audience,

00:49:07.298 --> 00:49:11.048
because I’m telling a lot about you.

00:49:11.551 --> 00:49:16.147
I think they will be interested to hear
your thoughts.

00:49:16.172 --> 00:49:18.797
I would be delighted.

00:49:19.599 --> 00:49:21.161
Great.

00:49:21.378 --> 00:49:25.609
So I wish you a nice day.
Here, it’s already evening.

00:49:25.634 --> 00:49:27.259
See you soon!

00:49:27.605 --> 00:49:29.980
Okay. All the best.

00:49:30.835 --> 00:49:34.110
So we’re wrapping up the video recording.

00:49:34.135 --> 00:49:38.959
You’ll have the audio,
because I’ve been recording this conversation.

00:49:38.984 --> 00:49:41.609
I’ll send you the second file as well.

00:49:42.750 --> 00:49:44.313
Okay.

00:49:44.401 --> 00:49:45.964
Thank you.

